        "text": "In geometric editing, the displacement is prescribed on the entirety of the boundary. However, we focus on studying the case where the prescribed displacement is local and the rest of the boundary is fixed akin to local control in classic representations. Deformation in Section 4.3 and smoothing in Section 4.4 are indirect examples of the target displacement being global.\nThe considered models are trained to fit the SDF and surface normals a shape and share the same architecture: sin activation functions and 3 hidden layers of 32 neurons each. In total, there are P = 2273 learnable parameters. We demonstrate several use-cases of geometric editing in Figure 1 with displacing parts of both man-made and organic neural implicit shapes. When the displacements are, loosely speaking, natural to the shape, the approximations recover the target well. However,\nnot arbitrarily complex displacements can be recovered as illustrated by the inscribed letters. The characteristic length of the detailed target is much finer than that of the shape, especially in that region. Certainly, the upper limit to how fine an arbitrary detail anywhere on the shape can be is given by A/P where A is the surface area of the shape. Furthermore, we hypothesize that the good memory-expressiveness trade-off of neural fields requires the NN to allocate geometric complexity where it is needed during training. To better illustrate this, some basis functions are shown in Figure 2. These do not encode obvious semantic or geometric meaning, but with increasing depth, the functions tend to become more complex. Nonetheless, the bases tend to have similar characteristic lengths as the shape itself."
