        "heading": "4.3 RIGID EDITING",
        "text": "As a last approach to editing, we briefly address deformation-rigidity since it classically is a widely used approach to shape editing. One the on hand, deformation energy is one of the many alternative priors to semantic prior discussed previously. A simple approach is to consider pure bending energy, since it can be computed only from the implicit representation itself. On the other hand, typical applications, such as as-rigid-as-possible (Sorkine & Alexa, 2007) or as-Killing-as-possible (Solomon et al., 2011), also include the stretching energy. This is not trivially applicable to implicit surfaces, as there is no natural notion of stretch due to ambiguity in the tangent directions. As discussed\nin Section 3, there are several choices to recovering tangential displacements, but we consider perhaps the simplest one \u2013 using another independent function to provide the tangential component \u03b4xt(x; \u039e) = ( I\u2212 n(x)n(x)\u22a4 ) ft(x; \u039e) as the tangential projection of a sufficiently smooth NN ft(x; \u039e) : R3 7\u2192 R3 parameterized in \u039e. We leverage Killing vector field (KVF) which generate isometric deformations. \u03b4x is a KVF if it has an anti-symmetric Jacobian J(\u03b4x) = J(\u03b4x)(x) \u2208 R3,3 everywhere on the boundary J(\u03b4x) + J(\u03b4x)\u22a4 = 0 \u2200x \u2208 \u0393 Solomon et al. (2011). A vector fields\u2019 deviation from being Killing can be measured with its Killing energy\nEK(\u03b4x,\u0393) = \u222b \u0393 \u2225J(\u03b4x) + J(\u03b4x)\u22a4\u22252 dx . (6)\nClassic works compute the Jacobians using discrete operators on spatial discretizations while Atzmon et al. (2021) formulates AKVF on a neural implicit directly. We follow this approach and seek an AKVF respecting the prescribed boundary deformations \u03b4x\u0304(xi).\nargmin \u03b4x EK + \u03b1EC (7)\nBoundary sensitivity allows to search for the energy minimizing deformation directly in the space of expressible deformations and directly