        "heading": "4.4 CONSTRAINTS",
        "text": "We demonstrate how boundary sensitivity can be used to fix a value of a functional during parameter updates. If the functional is expressed as a surface or volume integral, this can be done without computing the integrals themselves. An example of this is volume preservation, which can simulate incompressible material behaviour (Desbrun & Gascuel, 1995) and is desirable in smoothing to prevent shrinkage (Taubin, 1995). Fixing the volume of an implicit shape is difficult without going to an intermediate representation, such as a mesh (Remelli et al., 2020; Mehta et al., 2022), since there is no trivial way to differentiably compute the volume of an implicit shape.\nShape Derivatives To this end, we loosely introduce shape derivatives and refer to (Allaire et al., 2021) for more detail. H \u2032(\u2126)(\u03b4x) denotes a shape derivative of the functional H(\u2126) \u2208 R if the expansion H(\u2126\u03b4x) = H(\u2126) +H \u2032(\u2126)(\u03b4x) holds for small \u03b4x. We rewrite this using perturbations as \u03b4H(\u2126)(\u03b4x) = H(\u2126\u03b4x)\u2212H(\u2126). For several functionals the shape derivatives are known analytically. For the functional H(\u2126) = \u222b \u2126 hdx defined as a volume integral of h = h(x) \u2208 R the shape\nderivative is \u03b4H(\u2126)(\u03b4x) = \u222b \u2126 div (h\u03b4x) dx . To compute the volume H = V , we integrate over the multiplicative identity h = 1. Inserting the boundary sensitivity from Equation 2 we arrive at\n\u03b4V = \u03b4\u0398\u22a4 \u222b \u2126 b dx =: b\u22a4V \u03b4\u0398 (8)\nwhere bV = bV (x) \u2208 RP again acts as a basis, but for the volume. \u03b4V = 0 can now be enforced both as soft constraint or as a hard constraint by projecting any parameter update \u03b4\u0398 onto the RP\u22121 linear subspace of constant volume \u03b4\u0398\u03b4V=0 = (I\u2212 bV b\u22a4V )\u03b4\u0398.\nSmoothing without Shrinkage We demonstrate fixing the volume with smoothing. Smoothing on neural fields has been previously formulated using gradient descent on an objective penalizing the deviation from a specified mean-curvature (Yang et al., 2021) and as mean-curvature flow computed on an intermediate mesh Mehta et al. (2022). We also use mean-curvature flow without resorting to the intermediate mesh, but the proposed volume-constraint is agnostic to the choice of the smoothing method. For that, we first compute the mean curvature \u03ba(x) \u2208 R by expanding \u03ba(x) = div(n(x)). From there, we can reuse the geometric editing framework with the globally prescribed target displacement \u03b4xn(x) = \u2212\u03ba(x) \u2200 x \u2208 \u0393\u0304 = \u0393. Note, that this approach induces the correct geometric flow as discussed by Mehta et al. (2022). Figure 6 compares smoothing with and without this constraint. The volume after 86 iterations decreases by 1.6% with the constraint and 80% without. Note, that without a volume constraint the shape ultimately converges to a singular point.\nSimilarly, constant area can simulate the behaviour of unstretchable, but bendable materials, such as rope in 2D or textile in 3D. For this, one would leverage the shape derivative of the functional G(\u2126) =\u222b \u0393 g ds defined as a surface integral of g = g(x) \u2208 R \u03b4G(\u2126)(\u03b4x) = \u222b \u0393 (\u2202g/\u2202n+ \u03bag) \u03b4x\u22a4nds, where \u2202g/\u2202n is the directional derivative of g along n. More generally, a similar constraint can be formulated for any functional of the presented forms."
